WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.142
A metric measures distance and lets us define neighborhoods

00:00:04.142 --> 00:00:09.548
On the real line, the distance between two points is their absolute difference

00:00:09.548 --> 00:00:17.033
An open ball around a point consists of inputs closer than a chosen positive radius, excluding its boundary.

00:00:18.333 --> 00:00:24.694
A set is open when every member has some positive-radius ball staying entirely inside the set

00:00:24.694 --> 00:00:31.055
The point zero point eight has a small neighborhood inside the interval from zero to one

00:00:31.055 --> 00:00:34.633
Points closer to the edge may require smaller radii.

00:00:35.933 --> 00:00:42.339
The closed interval contains zero, but every full real-line ball around zero reaches negative values

00:00:42.339 --> 00:00:47.890
Therefore that interval is not open as a subset of the real line

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:51.733
Openness depends on the ambient space and its topology.

00:00:53.033 --> 00:01:01.050
Topology keeps the neighborhood idea through rules for open sets: arbitrary unions and finite intersections remain open

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:05.765
A metric supplies one way to obtain such a topology

00:01:05.765 --> 00:01:12.367
The abstract definition also allows spaces where no particular distance function has been chosen.
